From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: enable the GPU, at the operating point the vendor uses

`arm64-dts-a733-add-the-gpu-node-disabled` described the GPU and left it disabled, because two facts it depends on had
not been read on our own silicon. Both have been, on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-27, and
from a stack that shares no code with ours -- so this flips the node on and pins
the one thing that must not be left to chance.

WHAT SETTLED THE TWO RESERVATIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. The BVNC is no longer borrowed. The vendor DDK on this board's eMMC prints
   "PVR_K: Read BVNC 36.56.104.183 from HW device registers" at module init --
   a ROGUE_CR_CORE_ID read on this silicon, not a device-tree string. That closes
   the open BVNC question. The matching firmware
   image is Imagination's own open-stack file (freedesktop project 22015, branch
   powervr, WHENCE version 1.1.OS@6976702, LICENSE.powervr = Redistributable),
   pinned and hashed by the fetch-gpu-firmware stage (scripts/stages/gpu-firmware.sh);
   it is NOT the DDK blob, which is a different artefact of a different size and hash.
2. The two PCK-600 domain indices are corroborated twice: the vendor device tree
   on this board names pd_gpu_top_test -> 5 and pd_gpu_core_test -> 6, and the
   status registers (base + N * 0x1000 + 8) read 5 = 0x8 and 6 = 0x8 while the
   vendor driver holds the GPU, against 4 (the NPU) = 0x0 with the NPU idle and
   8 (USB2, the header's confirmed anchor) = 0x8.

THE ONE LINE THAT IS A SAFETY PROPERTY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
assigned-clock-parents pins GPU0_CLK's mux to PERI0_600M, and that is not a
preference.

Left alone, this clock comes out of reset on SEL=000 (GPUPLL) with the divider at
M=0, and PLL_GPU0's own reset value is N=70, P0=2 -- 840 MHz. VDD-GPU meanwhile
is whatever the boot chain left it at, and that was **measured**, not assumed:
DCDC4 reads 800 mV on this board (regulator_summary, 2026-08-27), and U-Boot does
not touch it -- the five rails it writes are swout1, swout2, bldo1, bldo2 and
dcdc2. The vendor's own OPP
table pairs 400 and 600 MHz with 800 mV, 800 MHz with 840 mV and 1008 MHz with
960 mV (allwinner-bsp configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi, gpu-opp-table). So
enabling this node without pinning the source would run the block at 840 MHz on
the 800 mV rail -- above the vendor's curve, with nothing in the tree to notice.

PERI0_600M with the divider left at reset is not a guess either: it is exactly
what the vendor stack runs on this silicon. Measured on 2026-08-27 under a real
GL load, GPU0_CLK read 0x82000000 -- gate on, SEL=010, M=0 -- with DCDC4 at
800 mV and the DDK reporting 600 MHz. 600 MHz at 800 mV is a row of the vendor
table, not an extrapolation from one.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- No assigned-clock-rates. gpu0 carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT and its parent here
  is a fixed factor off PLL_PERI0 -- the PLL that feeds the whole SoC. A rate
  request on gpu0 would propagate straight into it. Rate control belongs on the
  GPU's own PLL with an OPP consumer, which is the next milestone.
- No gpu-supply and no OPP table, therefore no regulator window on dcdc4 either.
  The tree's rule is that a window may only land together with the driver that
  consumes the rail; nothing here consumes it, so nothing here describes it.
- GPUPLL is not used, and could not serve this point anyway: the VCO window is
  1.26-2.52 GHz (UM Table 4-2), and 600 MHz would need
  24 MHz x 50 / 2, i.e. a 1200 MHz VCO, below the floor.
- ⚠ mainline's drm/powervr sets no rate of its own: pvr_power.c only calls
  clk_prepare_enable() on core, sys and mem (pvr_power.c:262-270), and "core" is
  the only mandatory one (pvr_device.c:103-115). So with no OPP table in the
  node, nothing in the kernel will move this clock.

The image side of this milestone is separate and in the same series: the module
set gains powervr.ko and the three DRM libraries it links against, and the
compose step installs the firmware together with LICENSE.powervr, because the
licence's one condition is that the notice travels with the binary.

VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- applies: patch -p1 --fuzz=0 in the full ordered series (local, 2026-08-27).
- builds: DTC 0 warnings.
- read back from the artefact: dtc -I dtb shows assigned-clocks <ccu 82> =
  CLK_GPU0, assigned-clock-parents <ccu 5> = CLK_PLL_PERI0_600M, status "okay".
- ✅ **HW-PROVEN 2026-09-07 on the 8 GiB board** -- all four falsifiers this
  patch fixed in advance were measured on the running board, none of them
  smoothed:
    GPU0_CLK 0x02002b20 = 0x82000000 -> SEL = 010, the pinned PERI0_600M parent
    clk framework: gpu0 enable=1 rate=600000000, pll-peri0-600m rate=600000000
    regulator_summary: dcdc4 = 800 mV
    the driver binds and loads its firmware: "Initialized powervr 1.0.0"
  Acceptance: a GPU test run, 7 passed / 0 failed.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -2731,39 +2731,28 @@
 		 * (pvr_drv.c dt_match[]); no SoC-specific string is needed and
 		 * none is invented here.
 		 *
-		 * ⚠ THE NODE IS DISABLED ON PURPOSE, and the two reasons are
-		 * named rather than left to be discovered:
+		 * ⚠ IT LANDED DISABLED AND IS ENABLED HERE, because both
+		 * reasons it waited on were settled on this board:
 		 *
-		 *   1. The firmware. drm/imagination loads a BVNC-named blob,
-		 *      and the BVNC this SoC needs is not established on our
-		 *      own silicon -- 36.56.104.183 comes from this project's
-		 *      earlier Armbian-based port, not from a ROGUE_CR_CORE_ID__PBVNC read here.
-		 *      Debian's
-		 *      firmware-misc-nonfree carries 36.53.104.796 and
-		 *      33.15.11.3, neither of which is that.
-		 *   2. The power domains below are UNVERIFIED indices. The
-		 *      PCK-600 header says so itself: only PD_USB2 = 8 is
-		 *      silicon-confirmed, the rest "follow the A733 hardware
-		 *      domain map". Attaching a live block to the wrong
-		 *      domain index power-manages a different block, so this
-		 *      node stays inert until both are read on a board.
-		 *
-		 * Falsifiers, both cheap once a board is in front of someone:
-		 * the domain status registers at 0x07060000 + N * 0x1000 + 0x8
-		 * (so 0x07065008 for TOP and 0x07066008 for CORE) must track
-		 * the GPU, and ROGUE_CR_CORE_ID__PBVNC must read the BVNC the
-		 * firmware file is named for.
-		 *
-		 * ⚠ power-domains IS EFFECTIVELY MANDATORY for this driver,
-		 * which the binding does not say. pvr_probe() calls
-		 * pvr_power_domains_init() before anything else, and that
-		 * function opens with of_count_phandle_with_args(...,
-		 * "power-domains", ...) and returns the count on a negative
-		 * value -- so a node WITHOUT the property fails probe with
-		 * -ENOENT. With exactly one domain it returns 0 early and the
-		 * platform core attaches it; with two it attaches them by the
-		 * names "a" and "b", which is what this SoC's TOP/CORE split
-		 * needs (pvr_power.c:607-660).
+		 *   1. The firmware. The BVNC is no longer a borrowed number:
+		 *      the vendor DDK on this board's eMMC prints "Read BVNC
+		 *      36.56.104.183 from HW device registers", i.e. a
+		 *      ROGUE_CR_CORE_ID read on this silicon, closing the
+		 *      open BVNC question. The matching
+		 *      file is Imagination's own open-stack image, pinned and
+		 *      hashed by the fetch-gpu-firmware build stage and installed
+		 *      into the image with its licence text.
+		 *   2. The power domains. The indices are corroborated twice:
+		 *      the vendor device tree names pd_gpu_top_test -> 5 and
+		 *      pd_gpu_core_test -> 6, and the PCK-600 status registers
+		 *      read 5 = 0x8 and 6 = 0x8 while the vendor driver holds
+		 *      the GPU, against 4 (the NPU) = 0x0 with the NPU idle.
+		 *
+		 * ⚠ What is deliberately NOT here yet is any rate control:
+		 * no OPP table, no gpu-supply, no devfreq. This node brings the
+		 * block up at one fixed, vendor-sanctioned operating point and
+		 * nothing more. Frequency and voltage move together or not at
+		 * all, and making them move is its own milestone.
 		 *
 		 * WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
 		 *
@@ -2799,7 +2788,42 @@
 			power-domains = <&pck600 PD_GPU_TOP>,
 					<&pck600 PD_GPU_CORE>;
 			power-domain-names = "a", "b";
-			status = "disabled";
+			/*
+			 * ⚠ THE PARENT IS PINNED AND THAT IS A SAFETY
+			 * PROPERTY, NOT A PREFERENCE. Left alone, GPU0_CLK
+			 * comes out of reset on SEL=000 (GPUPLL) with M=0,
+			 * and PLL_GPU0's own reset value is N=70 P0=2 --
+			 * 840 MHz. Meanwhile VDD-GPU is whatever the boot
+			 * chain left, measured 800 mV on this board, and the
+			 * vendor's own OPP table pairs 800 MHz with 840 mV
+			 * and 1008 MHz with 960 mV. Enabling this node
+			 * without pinning the source therefore runs the GPU
+			 * ABOVE the voltage curve, which is the one failure
+			 * mode that does not announce itself.
+			 *
+			 * PERI0_600M with the divider at its reset value is
+			 * exactly the operating point the vendor stack runs
+			 * on this silicon: measured 2026-08-27 under GL load,
+			 * GPU0_CLK = 0x82000000 (SEL=010, M=0) with DCDC4 at
+			 * 800 mV -- and 600 MHz at 800 mV is a row of the
+			 * vendor OPP table, not an extrapolation.
+			 *
+			 * ⚠ NO assigned-clock-rates, deliberately. gpu0
+			 * carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, and its parent here is
+			 * a fixed factor off PLL_PERI0 -- the PLL that feeds
+			 * the whole SoC. A rate request on gpu0 would
+			 * propagate straight into it. Rate control belongs to
+			 * an OPP consumer on the GPU's own PLL, which is the
+			 * next milestone, not to this line.
+			 *
+			 * ⚠ And GPUPLL cannot serve 600 MHz cleanly anyway:
+			 * the VCO window is 1.26-2.52 GHz (UM Table 4-2,
+			 * A733 user manual), so 24 MHz x 50 / 2 = 600 needs
+			 * a 1200 MHz VCO, below the floor.
+			 */
+			assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_GPU0>;
+			assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_600M>;
+			status = "okay";
 		};
 	};
 };
